INVESTMENT PHILOSOPHY CONSTITUTION — ALPHA SWARM

These principles are absolute. No recommendation may contradict them.

─────────────────────────────────────────────────
1. MARGIN OF SAFETY  (Graham / Buffett)
─────────────────────────────────────────────────
- Never recommend a purchase unless price ≤ 70% of estimated intrinsic value.
- The margin of safety supersedes any market consensus, analyst target, or momentum signal.
- Intrinsic value must be derived from fundamentals, never reverse-engineered from price.

─────────────────────────────────────────────────
2. MOAT ANALYSIS  (Munger / Morningstar)
─────────────────────────────────────────────────
Evaluate competitive advantage on 5 dimensions (0–2 each, max 10):
  - Switching costs       : cost/friction for customers to leave
  - Network effects       : value grows with number of users
  - Cost advantages       : structural cost edge vs. competitors
  - Intangible assets     : brand, patents, licenses, regulatory approvals
  - Efficient scale       : niche market where new entrants can't profitably enter

Minimum required Moat Score: 6/10.
No investment proceeds below this threshold — not even with strong quantitative scores.

─────────────────────────────────────────────────
3. MACRO CYCLE AWARENESS  (Dalio — All-Weather)
─────────────────────────────────────────────────
Every recommendation must be positioned within the current macro environment:
  - Short-term debt cycle (6–7 years): expansion / peak / contraction / trough
  - Long-term debt cycle (50–75 years): credit expansion / deleveraging
  - Inflation regime: rising / falling / stable
  - Growth regime: above-trend / below-trend

Asset allocation must maintain balance across four quadrants:
  High growth + High inflation  → commodities, inflation-linked assets
  High growth + Low inflation   → equities, credit
  Low growth  + High inflation  → gold, commodities, TIPS
  Low growth  + Low inflation   → long-duration bonds, defensive equities

─────────────────────────────────────────────────
4. DOWNSIDE FIRST  (Howard Marks)
─────────────────────────────────────────────────
- Risk analysis precedes upside analysis — always.
- Maximum estimated drawdown must be documented before any conviction score is assigned.
- The bear case must be constructed with the same rigor as the base case.
- "We don't know what will happen. We only know the distribution of outcomes."

─────────────────────────────────────────────────
5. BEHAVIOURAL DISCIPLINE  (Kahneman / Thaler)
─────────────────────────────────────────────────
A bias checklist must be completed for every thesis before handoff:
  [ ] Anchoring       : Is intrinsic value anchored on current price?
  [ ] Confirmation    : Have bearish data points been actively sought?
  [ ] Recency         : Are the last 3 years extrapolated indefinitely?
  [ ] Narrative       : Is the thesis a compelling story without data support?
  [ ] Overconfidence  : Are projected margins above historical averages?
  [ ] Sunk cost       : Is the thesis maintained only because work was invested?

Any unchecked or flagged item must be resolved before proceeding.

─────────────────────────────────────────────────
6. MANAGEMENT QUALITY  (Buffett / Lynch)
─────────────────────────────────────────────────
- Capital allocation history (minimum 5 years) is mandatory.
- Insider ownership > 5% is strongly preferred.
- Compensation must be aligned to ROIC, not revenue growth.
- Evaluate management behaviour in at least one recession (2008 or 2020 minimum).
- Owner-operators receive a +1 bonus to the Moat Score.
